# Routing Relationship Notes

This note starts at `src/http-server/src/Response.php`. At this stage, it is only an observation point for Response on the HTTP side: routing relationships aren't laid out as a full diagram here. First, keep track of the existing conventions around the response object, so later reading doesn't mistake the filename itself for a registration entry point.

Four imports are visible at the top of the file:

```php
use BadMethodCallException;
use Hyperf\Context\Context;
use Hyperf\Contract\Arrayable;
use Hyperf\Contract\Jsonable;
```

`Context` appears alongside two contracts, indicating that reading the response object here will involve context and two representation conventions: arrays and JSON. `BadMethodCallException` is a name to revisit when looking further into exception exit points. Record `Arrayable` and `Jsonable` separately; don't lump them together as a single output convention.

Two gaps to fill: where Response is constructed, and how Context participates in response handling in this file. Once these two points are filled in, decide whether "routing relationships" should be split out into another note.

# Adapter Entry Point Review Guide

Start with `src/etcd/src/V3/EtcdClient.php`. This file is in etcd's V3 directory and was treated at the time as the location of the Hyperf-side adapter entry point: first check what the file header imports, then follow the actual usage sites to see where requests are assembled.

There is just one direct dependency in the file header worth noting first:

```php
use GuzzleHttp\Client as HttpClient;
```

The initial assessment was that the alias `HttpClient` kept the HTTP client within the local scope of EtcdClient.php. On the first read, this was taken as a sign that "all requests are sent from this file"; that reading is sufficient as a navigation aid at this stage, but the construction and call sites have not yet been connected.

Keep the review within the directory to start:

1. Within EtcdClient.php, find occurrences of `HttpClient` and distinguish type-only use, stored instances, and direct calls;
2. If the client is passed in as a parameter, continue recording where it is passed in; don't infer the construction site from the import;
3. If the client is created within the file, note the configuration source near its creation;
4. Start documenting return values and exceptions at the point closest to the request; don't speculate about the event side here yet.

The path itself only shows that this belongs to etcd's V3 code layer. The earlier interpretation of the directory name as the full Hyperf adapter boundary was written too hastily; a follow-up review needs to check which statements `HttpClient` actually appears in. Failure and event exit points have not yet been recorded.

# Channel and Composer Migration Workflow

First, note the old investigation starting point: `src/pool/src/Channel.php`. At the time, we traced back from the terms channel, coroutine, and size. The default assumption was that this file would still handle the lifecycle after the component split. The dependencies we saw at the top of the file were:

```php
use Hyperf\Contract\ConnectionInterface;
use Hyperf\Engine\Channel as CoChannel;
use Hyperf\Utils\Coroutine;
use SplQueue;
```

Leave these items unchanged for now. Channel creation, closing, and wait relationships haven't been matched up one by one yet; the composer metadata note also only records that an old call couldn't find the entry point. Don't remove the old path yet during the migration. Still needed: the new component entry point, the actual callers, and whether we can fall back to the old directory on failure.
